  "query_preview": "..\n\nTransition day in the upper levels as we go from split flow pattern \ninto an amplified blocking pattern with Plains ridge in between \ncoastal troughs. In getting there, we'll be presented with the only \nchance for precipitation for about the next week, and even that \nchance is only for the far southern locations mainly south of I94 \ntonight into Wednesday. Cause of this rain chance will be an upper \nlow now over northern IL which will drift eastward in the weak flow \non the south side of the split flow pattern while phasing with an \nelongated trough dropping out of the northern split and also reside \nweak troughing currently over the east coast. Models have been \nconsistently showing  a narrow axis of low level convergence around \nLake Erie reaching into southern MI concurrent with mid level \ndeformation north of the low and with a plume of enhanced theta e \nretrograding west around the north side of the low. Saturated layer \nin model sounding still up to around 10kft tonight but now extends \nfurther to around 15kft during the day Wednesday. Moisture does \nbecome more elevated with developing dry layer in the low level so \nchances of rain extending into Wednesday afternoon are low. High \nchance pop low QPF event for southern MI Tuesday night with less \nthan a tenth of liquid for those that see any rain.  \n\nHeading into the weekend, the next longwave trough will come ashore \nthe Pacific NW causing the Plains ridge to amplify setting up the \nblocking pattern across the conus. The expansive surface ridge to \nthe north keeps easterly flow through the weekend allowing for only \nslowly moderating temperatures with the thermal trough holding over \nthe region. So highs in the 60s will carry through the weekend \nbefore the ridge axis reaches lower MI allowing for southerly flow \nto return boosting temps back into the low 70s. With good amount of \nclear skies at night, favorable radiational cooling setup will allow \nlows to drop into the 40s each night.",
